## Why This Is Asked
Concurrency is central to both Discord-like chat and Snowflake-like storage. This question probes ability to map a real production pattern (per-channel queues) to concrete synchronization, backpressure, and ordering guarantees.

## Key Concepts
- Producer-consumer pattern
- Bounded buffers and backpressure
- Mutexes and condition variables
- Per-channel ordering and fault handling

## Code Example

```javascript
class BoundedQueue {
  constructor(capacity) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.buf = new Array(capacity);
    this.head = 0;
    this.tail = 0;
    this.size = 0;
    this.mux = new Mutex();
    this.notFull = new ConditionVariable();
    this.notEmpty = new ConditionVariable();
  }
  enqueue(item) {
    // acquire mutex; wait while size === capacity; store at tail; tail=(tail+1)%capacity; size++; notEmpty.signal();
  }
  dequeue() {
    // acquire mutex; wait while size === 0; read from head; head=(head+1)%capacity; size--; notFull.signal();
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test backpressure and ordering across bursty producers?
- How would you extend to multiple consumers while preserving per-channel order?

## Why This Is Asked
Interleaves real-world concurrency challenges: per-key in-order guarantees, bounded buffers, dynamic worker pools, and backpressure under bursts. Tests deep understanding of ordering, backpressure signaling, and load repartitioning.

## Key Concepts
- Per-key (cell) in-order processing
- Bounded per-key FIFOs
- Dispatcher with stable routing (consistent hashing)
- Per-key reordering buffers
- Global in-flight limiter with backpressure and dynamic rebalance

## Code Example
```javascript
class CellState {
  constructor() {
    this.lastSeq = 0;
    thisbuffer = new Map(); // cell_id -> [msgs in order]
  }
  handle(msg){
    const {cell_id, seq} = msg;
    const s = this.state.get(cell_id) || {lastSeq:0, buf:[]};
    if(seq === s.lastSeq+1){
      s.lastSeq = seq;
      this.emit(msg);
      // drain buffered contiguous msgs
    } else {
      s.buf.push(msg);
      // keep bounded and sort by seq for reordering
    }
    this.state.set(cell_id, s);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test correctness when late events arrive after many already-processed messages?
- What backpressure signals would you expose to upstream systems and how would you prevent starvation during rebalance?

